// New articles, 18 November 2026 (part 9): technology.
ADDART({id:"worldwideweb",t:"The World Wide Web",c:"Technology",i:"🌐",w:"World Wide Web",r:["internet","searchengines","email","socialmedia","comp"],
p:[["Pages linked together","The World Wide Web is a system of linked pages viewed through browsers."],["Invented at CERN","Tim Berners-Lee proposed the Web in 1989."],["Not the same as the internet","The Web runs on top of the internet, which it made popular."]],
l:"The World Wide Web is an information system of documents and other resources, linked by hyperlinks and identified by web addresses, that people access over the internet using web browsers. It was invented by British computer scientist Tim Berners-Lee at CERN, the European physics laboratory near Geneva, who proposed it in March 1989 to help scientists share information. By the end of 1990 he had written the first web browser, the first web server and the first web page, along with the three key technologies still used today: HTML, the language for writing pages; URLs, the addresses of pages; and HTTP, the rules for transferring them. CERN made the Web’s technology freely available in April 1993, and the Mosaic browser, released the same year, displayed images alongside text and helped the Web spread explosively. The Web is often confused with the internet, the worldwide network of computers that also carries email, messaging and other services. Today there are more than a billion websites, and the Web is central to news, commerce, education, entertainment and government.",
s:[["How the Web works","When you type a web address or click a link, your browser asks a server, using HTTP, for a page. The server sends back HTML, along with style sheets (CSS) that control appearance and JavaScript programs that add interactivity. The browser assembles them into the page you see. HTTPS encrypts the connection."],["The first website","The first website, info.cern.ch, explained the Web itself and went online in 1991. CERN restored it in 2013. The first web server was a NeXT computer with a handwritten label warning: ‘This machine is a server. DO NOT POWER IT DOWN!!’"],["Browser wars","Netscape Navigator dominated the mid-1990s until Microsoft bundled Internet Explorer with Windows, leading to a famous US antitrust case. Mozilla Firefox (2004) and Google Chrome (2008) later took over; Chrome became the most used browser."],["Web 2.0 and beyond","In the 2000s, ‘Web 2.0’ described sites where users create content, such as blogs, Wikipedia, YouTube and social networks. Concerns today include privacy, misinformation, the power of a few large platforms and keeping the Web open and accessible."]],
k:[["Inventor","Tim Berners-Lee"],["Proposed","March 1989, at CERN"],["Key technologies","HTML, URLs, HTTP"],["Made free","April 1993"],["Websites","More than a billion"]],
d:[["1989","Tim Berners-Lee proposes the Web"],["1990","First web browser and server"],["1991","Web made available outside CERN"],["1993","CERN releases the Web free; Mosaic browser launched"],["1994","World Wide Web Consortium (W3C) founded"],["2008","Google Chrome launched"]],
s2:[["‘Vague but exciting’","Berners-Lee’s boss at CERN, Mike Sendall, wrote ‘Vague but exciting…’ on the first proposal for the Web, allowing him to continue working on it."],["‘This is for everyone’","At the opening ceremony of the 2012 London Olympics, Tim Berners-Lee appeared at a computer and tweeted ‘This is for everyone’, celebrating the decision to keep the Web free."]],
sp:["Internet vs Web","The internet is the network of cables, routers and computers, created in the 1960s–1980s. The Web is one service that runs on it, like email or video calls. The Web made the internet easy to use for ordinary people."],
g:[["Hyperlink","A clickable link to another page"],["Browser","Software for viewing web pages"],["HTML","The language used to write web pages"],["URL","A web address"]],
f:["Tim Berners-Lee invented the Web in 1989.","The Web and the internet are not the same.","CERN made the Web free in 1993."],
fun:"Tim Berners-Lee later said that if he could change one thing, he would remove the two slashes after ‘http:’, which he said were unnecessary."});

ADDART({id:"soundrecording",t:"Sound Recording",c:"Technology",i:"💿",w:"Sound recording and reproduction",r:["headphones","microphones","rockpop","streaming","radio","vinylrevival"],
p:[["Capturing sound","Sound recording stores sound so it can be played back."],["From cylinders to streams","Formats moved from wax cylinders and vinyl to tapes, CDs and digital files."],["Changing music","Recording turned music into something people could own and share."]],
l:"Sound recording is the capture of sound waves so that they can be stored and reproduced later. In 1857, French inventor Édouard-Léon Scott de Martinville built the phonautograph, which traced sound waves on paper but could not play them back. In 1877, Thomas Edison invented the phonograph, which recorded sound as grooves on a tin-foil cylinder and played it back; his first recording was ‘Mary Had a Little Lamb’. Emile Berliner introduced flat discs, the gramophone records, in 1887. Electrical recording with microphones arrived in 1925. Magnetic tape, developed in Germany in the 1930s, allowed editing and multitrack recording, transforming music production. The 33⅓ rpm long-playing vinyl record appeared in 1948, compact cassettes in 1963 and the digital compact disc (CD) in 1982. In the 1990s, the MP3 format compressed music into small digital files, enabling file sharing and the iPod; since the 2010s, streaming has become the main way people listen. Vinyl records have made a surprising comeback among collectors.",
s:[["Analog recording","Analog recordings store sound as continuous physical patterns: wavy grooves on records or changing magnetism on tape. Playback converts these patterns back into vibrations. Records and tapes can wear out, crackle or hiss."],["Digital recording","Digital recording measures sound waves thousands of times a second, 44,100 times for CDs, and stores the measurements as numbers. Copies are identical, and digital editing became easy. Compression formats like MP3 discard sounds the ear is unlikely to notice to make files smaller."],["The studio","Multitrack tape let musicians record parts separately and layer them. The Beatles’ Sgt. Pepper’s Lonely Hearts Club Band (1967) showed what studios could do. Today, music can be recorded and produced on a laptop with digital audio workstation software."],["The vinyl revival","Vinyl record sales, nearly extinct by the 2000s, grew for more than 15 years in a row from the mid-2000s. In the United States, vinyl outsold CDs in 2020 for the first time since the 1980s. Fans value the large artwork, the ritual of playing records and their sound."]],
k:[["First playback","Edison’s phonograph, 1877"],["Flat disc records","Emile Berliner, 1887"],["LP vinyl","1948"],["Compact disc","1982"],["Main way to listen today","Streaming"]],
d:[["1857","Phonautograph traces sound waves"],["1877","Edison invents the phonograph"],["1887","Berliner’s gramophone disc"],["1925","Electrical recording begins"],["1948","LP record introduced"],["1963","Compact cassette introduced"],["1982","Compact disc launched"],["2001","Apple’s iPod launched"]],
s2:[["The oldest recording","In 2008, researchers used computers to play a phonautograph tracing made by Scott de Martinville in 1860, a voice singing ‘Au clair de la lune’, making it the oldest known recording of a human voice, 17 years before Edison."],["The Walkman and the iPod","Sony’s Walkman (1979) made music portable, and Apple’s iPod (2001) promised ‘1,000 songs in your pocket’. Smartphones and streaming services later replaced both."]],
sp:["Napster","Napster, launched in 1999 by student Shawn Fanning, let millions share MP3 files for free. Record companies sued, and it shut down in 2001, but it changed the music industry and led to paid downloads and streaming."],
g:[["Analog","Stored as a continuous physical pattern"],["Digital","Stored as numbers"],["Multitrack","Recording separate parts to combine later"],["Compression","Making digital files smaller"]],
f:["Edison’s first recording was ‘Mary Had a Little Lamb’.","CDs measure sound 44,100 times a second.","Vinyl outsold CDs in the US in 2020."],
fun:"The oldest playable recording of a human voice, from 1860, was never meant to be heard; it was a tracing on paper that computers turned into sound in 2008."});

ADDART({id:"cgi",t:"CGI and Visual Effects",c:"Technology",i:"🎬",w:"Computer-generated imagery",r:["anim","cinema","videogames","dino","vrar"],
p:[["Images from computers","CGI creates images and scenes using computer graphics."],["Movie magic","Visual effects bring dinosaurs, aliens and impossible worlds to the screen."],["From Tron to today","CGI went from simple shapes to photorealistic worlds."]],
l:"Computer-generated imagery (CGI) is the use of computer graphics to create images, animations and visual effects in films, television, video games, advertising and simulations. Visual effects (VFX) combine CGI with live-action footage to show things that would be impossible, dangerous or too expensive to film. Early milestones include the computer-animated sequences in Westworld (1973), the glowing world of Tron (1982) and the first fully CGI character, the stained-glass knight in Young Sherlock Holmes (1985). Terminator 2 (1991) featured a liquid-metal villain, and Jurassic Park (1993) amazed audiences with realistic dinosaurs. In 1995, Pixar’s Toy Story became the first fully computer-animated feature film. Motion capture, which records actors’ movements to animate digital characters, brought Gollum in The Lord of the Rings and the Na’vi of Avatar to life. Today, virtual production uses huge LED screens displaying computer-generated backgrounds in real time, and artificial intelligence tools are changing visual effects work, raising concerns among artists and actors.",
s:[["How CGI is made","Artists build 3D models from digital meshes, add textures and materials, set up virtual lights and cameras, animate the models and then ‘render’ the scenes, calculating how light bounces to produce final images. A single frame of a complex film can take many hours to render on large computer farms."],["Motion capture","Actors wear suits with markers or sensors, and cameras record their movements, which are applied to digital characters. Performance capture also records facial expressions. Andy Serkis became famous for performances such as Gollum and Caesar in Planet of the Apes."],["Virtual production","For The Mandalorian (2019), filmmakers surrounded actors with giant LED walls showing computer-generated landscapes that moved with the camera, letting actors see their surroundings and capturing realistic lighting in camera."],["Debates","Critics argue that overuse of CGI can make films look artificial, and many directors combine CGI with practical effects. Visual effects artists often work long hours under tight deadlines. The use of AI to generate images and digital replicas of actors was a major issue in Hollywood strikes in 2023."]],
k:[["Stands for","Computer-generated imagery"],["First CGI character","Young Sherlock Holmes (1985)"],["Landmark dinosaurs","Jurassic Park (1993)"],["First CGI feature film","Toy Story (1995)"],["Key technique","Motion capture"]],
d:[["1973","Westworld uses digital image processing"],["1982","Tron features extensive CGI"],["1993","Jurassic Park’s CGI dinosaurs"],["1995","Toy Story released"],["2002","Gollum created with performance capture"],["2009","Avatar released"],["2019","Virtual production with LED walls"]],
s2:[["Jurassic Park’s dinosaurs","Jurassic Park originally planned to use stop-motion dinosaurs. After animators at Industrial Light & Magic showed a test of a computer-generated T. rex, director Steven Spielberg switched to CGI, changing filmmaking."],["The uncanny valley","Digital humans that look almost, but not quite, real can seem eerie, an effect called the uncanny valley. Films such as The Polar Express (2004) were criticized for it."]],
sp:["Render farms","Pixar’s Toy Story took about 800,000 machine hours to render. Modern films can require hundreds of millions of hours spread across thousands of computers."],
g:[["Render","To calculate the final image from a 3D scene"],["Motion capture","Recording movement to animate digital characters"],["Visual effects","Imagery added to or changed in footage"],["Uncanny valley","Unease at almost-human figures"]],
f:["Toy Story was the first fully CGI feature film.","Jurassic Park switched from stop-motion to CGI.","Actors use motion capture to play digital characters."],
fun:"The water droplet in the computer-generated ‘pseudopod’ in The Abyss (1989) was one of the first convincing CGI effects, paving the way for Terminator 2’s liquid-metal villain."});

ADDART({id:"holograms",t:"Holograms",c:"Technology",i:"✨",w:"Holography",r:["laser","light","money","vrar","particles"],
p:[["3D images from light","A hologram records light to create a three-dimensional image."],["Invented in 1947","Dennis Gabor invented holography and won a Nobel Prize."],["On your bank card","Holograms help prevent counterfeiting of money and cards."]],
l:"A hologram is a recording of light that can recreate a three-dimensional image of an object, which appears to have depth and changes as the viewer moves. Holography was invented in 1947 by Hungarian-British physicist Dennis Gabor, who won the 1971 Nobel Prize in Physics for it. Practical holograms became possible only after the invention of the laser in 1960, which produces the pure, coherent light needed. To make a hologram, a laser beam is split in two: one beam lights the object and reflects onto a photographic plate, while the other shines directly onto the plate; the two beams interfere, creating a complex pattern that stores information about the light’s brightness and direction. When the plate is lit correctly, it reconstructs the light waves, and the viewer sees a 3D image. Holograms are used as security features on banknotes, credit cards, passports and product packaging because they are hard to copy, as well as in art and data storage. Many so-called ‘holograms’ in concerts and films are actually optical illusions such as Pepper’s ghost.",
s:[["How it works","Unlike a photograph, which records only brightness, a hologram records the interference pattern of light waves, capturing their phase, or timing, as well. This is why a hologram can show different views of an object from different angles. Cutting a hologram in half still shows the whole object, though with less detail."],["Security holograms","Shimmering rainbow holograms on banknotes, cards and documents are made by embossing patterns into foil. They are very difficult to reproduce accurately, making forgery harder. The euro, US dollar and many other currencies include holographic features."],["‘Holograms’ that aren’t","Performances by deceased musicians, such as Tupac Shakur at the Coachella festival in 2012, used an updated version of Pepper’s ghost, a 19th-century theater illusion that reflects an image onto glass. Science fiction holograms floating in midair, like Princess Leia’s message in Star Wars, remain largely fictional."],["Future uses","Researchers are developing holographic displays for 3D television, car head-up displays, medical imaging and augmented reality glasses. Holographic data storage could store large amounts of data in three dimensions inside crystals."]],
k:[["Invented by","Dennis Gabor, 1947"],["Nobel Prize","1971"],["Needed light source","Laser"],["Common use","Security features"],["Common illusion","Pepper’s ghost"]],
d:[["1862","Pepper’s ghost illusion shown in London"],["1947","Dennis Gabor invents holography"],["1960","Laser invented"],["1962","First laser holograms made"],["1971","Gabor wins the Nobel Prize"],["1980s","Holograms appear on credit cards"]],
s2:[["The first laser holograms","In 1962, Emmett Leith and Juris Upatnieks in the United States and Yuri Denisyuk in the Soviet Union independently made the first laser holograms, including a hologram of a toy train."],["Museum of Holography","The Museum of Holography in New York, open from 1976 to 1992, displayed holographic art. Its collection was later moved to the MIT Museum."]],
sp:["Pepper’s ghost","Pepper’s ghost, popularized by John Henry Pepper in London in 1862, uses angled glass to reflect a hidden, brightly lit figure, making it appear as a transparent ghost on stage. It is still used in theme park rides and teleprompters."],
g:[["Hologram","A recording that recreates a 3D image with light"],["Interference","Combining waves to form patterns"],["Coherent light","Light waves in step, like a laser"],["Counterfeiting","Making fake copies"]],
f:["Dennis Gabor invented holography in 1947.","Holograms need lasers to make.","Many concert ‘holograms’ are optical illusions."],
fun:"If you cut a true hologram in half, each half still shows the entire image, just from a narrower range of angles."});

ADDART({id:"machinetranslation",t:"Machine Translation",c:"Technology",i:"🈂️",w:"Machine translation",r:["llm","langevo","ai","neural","mlearn"],
p:[["Computers that translate","Machine translation uses software to translate between languages."],["From rules to neural networks","Methods moved from grammar rules to statistics and neural networks."],["Useful but imperfect","It helps billions of people but can make serious mistakes."]],
l:"Machine translation is the use of computer software to translate text or speech from one language to another. In 1954, the Georgetown–IBM experiment publicly translated more than 60 Russian sentences into English, and researchers predicted that the problem would be solved within a few years. It proved far harder: early systems used dictionaries and hand-written grammar rules and struggled with ambiguity, idioms and context. In the 1990s and 2000s, statistical machine translation learned patterns from huge collections of translated texts; Google Translate, launched in 2006, used this approach. In 2016, Google switched to neural machine translation, using artificial neural networks that translate whole sentences at once and produce much more fluent results. Large language models now also translate well. Machine translation is used billions of times a day for websites, messages, travel and business, and real-time speech translation is built into phones and earbuds. However, it can make errors with rare languages, culture, humor, specialized terms and gender, so professional human translators are still needed for legal, medical and literary work.",
s:[["Why translation is hard","Words have multiple meanings, and grammar differs widely between languages. A famous example: ‘The spirit is willing, but the flesh is weak’ was said to have been translated into Russian and back as ‘The vodka is good, but the meat is rotten’, though this story is probably a legend."],["Statistical and neural approaches","Statistical systems broke sentences into phrases and calculated likely translations from parallel texts such as United Nations and European Parliament documents. Neural systems learn representations of meaning, considering the whole sentence, which improved fluency and handling of word order."],["Low-resource languages","Many of the world’s roughly 7,000 languages have few digital texts, so translation quality is poor. Projects such as Meta’s No Language Left Behind (2022) and Google’s expansion to more than 240 languages in 2024 aimed to include more languages, often with help from native speakers."],["Translators and machines","Many professional translators now edit machine output, called post-editing. Mistakes in machine translation have caused problems in hospitals, courts and asylum cases, so experts warn against relying on it for important decisions without human checks."]],
k:[["First public demonstration","Georgetown–IBM, 1954"],["Google Translate launched","2006"],["Neural translation","From 2016"],["Languages in Google Translate","More than 240 (2024)"],["Human role","Checking and post-editing"]],
d:[["1954","Georgetown–IBM experiment"],["1966","ALPAC report cuts US funding for translation research"],["1990s","Statistical machine translation develops"],["2006","Google Translate launched"],["2016","Neural machine translation introduced"],["2024","Google Translate adds 110 languages"]],
s2:[["The ALPAC report","In 1966, a US government committee concluded that machine translation was slower, less accurate and more expensive than human translation, leading to sharp funding cuts for a decade."],["Babel fish","In Douglas Adams’s The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy, a ‘Babel fish’ placed in the ear translates any language instantly. The name was later used for an early online translation service."]],
sp:["Speech translation","Real-time speech translation combines speech recognition, machine translation and speech synthesis. Some earbuds and phones can now translate conversations almost instantly, though accuracy varies with accents and background noise."],
g:[["Parallel text","A text and its translation side by side"],["Neural network","A computer system modeled loosely on the brain"],["Post-editing","Correcting machine translation"],["Idiom","A phrase whose meaning is not literal"]],
f:["The first public machine translation was in 1954.","Google Translate switched to neural networks in 2016.","Many languages still lack good machine translation."],
fun:"Early researchers in 1954 predicted that machine translation would be solved in three to five years; it took more than 60 years to become fluent."});

ADDART({id:"facialrecognition",t:"Facial Recognition",c:"Technology",i:"🙂",w:"Facial recognition system",r:["surveillance","ai","dataprivacy","compvision","smartphone"],
p:[["Identifying faces","Facial recognition software identifies or checks people from images of their faces."],["Everyday uses","It unlocks phones, speeds airport checks and tags photos."],["Controversial","Critics warn about surveillance, bias and privacy."]],
l:"Facial recognition is technology that identifies or verifies a person by analyzing the features of their face in a photo or video. Software detects a face, measures features such as the distance between the eyes and the shape of the nose and jaw, converts them into a numerical ‘faceprint’ and compares it with stored images. Early work began in the 1960s, when Woody Bledsoe manually marked facial features for computers to compare. Accuracy improved dramatically in the 2010s with deep learning, and the best systems now match faces with very high accuracy under good conditions. Facial recognition unlocks smartphones, verifies identities at airport gates and banks, tags friends in photos and helps police search for suspects and missing people. It is also used for mass surveillance, notably in China. Critics raise concerns about privacy, the loss of anonymity in public, wrongful arrests based on false matches and studies showing higher error rates for women and people with darker skin in some systems. Several cities have banned police use, and the European Union’s AI Act restricts real-time facial recognition in public spaces.",
s:[["Verification and identification","Verification checks whether a person is who they claim to be, comparing one face with one stored image, as when unlocking a phone. Identification searches a database to find who a person is, which raises bigger privacy concerns."],["Phones","Apple introduced Face ID with the iPhone X in 2017, projecting about 30,000 infrared dots to map the face in 3D, making it hard to fool with a photo. Many phones now use similar systems."],["Bias","A 2018 study by Joy Buolamwini and Timnit Gebru found that commercial systems misidentified darker-skinned women far more often than lighter-skinned men. A 2019 US government study confirmed differences across many algorithms, though the best systems have improved."],["Rules","San Francisco banned city agencies, including police, from using facial recognition in 2019. The EU AI Act, whose bans took effect in February 2025, prohibits most real-time remote facial recognition by police in public spaces and the scraping of faces from the internet to build databases."]],
k:[["Works by","Comparing faceprints"],["Early research","Woody Bledsoe, 1960s"],["Face ID","iPhone X, 2017"],["Concerns","Privacy, surveillance, bias"],["EU rules","AI Act bans from February 2025"]],
d:[["1964","Woody Bledsoe begins facial recognition research"],["2001","Facial recognition used at the Super Bowl in Tampa"],["2014","Deep learning brings near-human accuracy"],["2017","Face ID introduced on smartphones"],["2018","Study reveals racial and gender bias"],["2019","San Francisco bans police facial recognition"],["2025","EU AI Act bans take effect"]],
s2:[["Clearview AI","The company Clearview AI built a database of billions of faces scraped from the internet and sold searches to police. Regulators in several countries fined it or ordered it to delete data of their citizens."],["Wrongful arrests","In the United States, several people have been wrongfully arrested after facial recognition falsely matched them to suspects, including Robert Williams in Detroit in 2020, leading to new police policies."]],
sp:["Fooling the cameras","Artists and activists have designed makeup patterns, glasses and clothing to confuse facial recognition systems, though newer systems are harder to fool."],
g:[["Faceprint","A numerical description of a face"],["Verification","Confirming identity"],["Identification","Finding who someone is"],["Deep learning","AI using many-layered neural networks"]],
f:["Face ID uses about 30,000 infrared dots.","Some systems were less accurate for darker-skinned women.","San Francisco banned police facial recognition in 2019."],
fun:"Some airports now let passengers board planes simply by looking at a camera, without showing a boarding pass or passport."});

ADDART({id:"datastorage",t:"Data Storage",c:"Technology",i:"💾",w:"Computer data storage",r:["comp","chip","cloud","datacenters","soundrecording"],
p:[["Keeping information","Data storage devices keep digital information for later use."],["From punch cards to flash","Storage moved from punched cards and tape to disks and chips."],["Shrinking and growing","Devices got smaller while storage capacity grew enormously."]],
l:"Data storage is the recording of digital information so that computers can keep and retrieve it. Early computers stored data on punched cards and paper tape, a technology dating back to the Jacquard loom of 1804 and Herman Hollerith’s tabulating machines for the 1890 US census. Magnetic tape arrived in the 1950s, and in 1956 IBM introduced the first hard disk drive, the RAMAC 350, which was the size of two refrigerators and held about 5 megabytes. Floppy disks, introduced in 1971, let people carry data between computers; the 3.5-inch floppy held 1.44 megabytes. Optical discs such as CDs, DVDs and Blu-ray store data as tiny pits read by lasers. Flash memory, invented at Toshiba in the 1980s, stores data in chips without moving parts and is used in USB drives, memory cards, smartphones and solid-state drives (SSDs). Today, much data is stored in cloud data centers. Storage capacity has grown by about a billion times since the 1950s, while costs per gigabyte have fallen dramatically. Researchers are even testing storage in DNA and glass.",
s:[["Hard disk drives","A hard drive stores data magnetically on spinning platters, read by heads floating nanometers above the surface. Modern hard drives hold more than 30 terabytes and remain the cheapest way to store huge amounts of data in data centers."],["Flash and SSDs","Flash memory traps electric charges in tiny cells to store bits. Solid-state drives are much faster than hard drives, silent and resistant to shocks, and have replaced hard drives in most laptops. They wear out slowly after many writes."],["Units","A byte is 8 bits. A kilobyte is about a thousand bytes, a megabyte a million, a gigabyte a billion, a terabyte a trillion and a petabyte a quadrillion. A typical smartphone photo is a few megabytes."],["Long-term storage","Digital data can become unreadable when formats and devices become obsolete, a problem called the ‘digital dark age’. Archives migrate data regularly. Experimental methods such as Microsoft’s Project Silica store data in glass that could last thousands of years, and DNA can store enormous amounts of data in a tiny space."]],
k:[["First hard drive","IBM RAMAC 350, 1956 (about 5 MB)"],["Floppy disk","1971"],["Flash memory","Invented at Toshiba, 1980s"],["Fast modern storage","Solid-state drives"],["Experimental","DNA and glass storage"]],
d:[["1890","Hollerith’s punched cards used for the US census"],["1956","First hard disk drive"],["1971","Floppy disk introduced"],["1982","Compact disc introduced"],["1987","NAND flash memory invented"],["2000","USB flash drives go on sale"]],
s2:[["The save icon","The ‘save’ icon in many programs still shows a 3.5-inch floppy disk, though many young users have never seen one. Some recognize it only as a symbol."],["Moving the RAMAC","The first hard drive was so large that it had to be moved by forklift and transported in planes. It was leased for about 3,200 dollars a month rather than sold."]],
sp:["DNA storage","In 2012, scientists encoded a whole book in DNA. Theoretically, a gram of DNA could store about 215 petabytes. DNA storage is still slow and expensive, but it could preserve data for thousands of years."],
g:[["Byte","A unit of 8 bits of data"],["Hard drive","A device storing data on spinning magnetic disks"],["Flash memory","Chip-based storage without moving parts"],["SSD","Solid-state drive"]],
f:["The first hard drive held about 5 megabytes.","Flash memory has no moving parts.","DNA can store huge amounts of data."],
fun:"A modern microSD card the size of a fingernail can hold more than 200,000 times the data of the first 1-ton hard drive."});

ADDART({id:"operatingsystems",t:"Operating Systems",c:"Technology",i:"🪟",w:"Operating system",r:["comp","smartphone","opensource","cybersecurity","coding","emulators"],
p:[["The computer’s manager","An operating system manages a computer’s hardware and runs programs."],["Windows, macOS, Linux","These are the main desktop operating systems."],["Phones too","Android and iOS run almost all smartphones."]],
l:"An operating system (OS) is the core software that manages a computer’s hardware and software resources and provides services for programs. It controls memory, processors, storage and devices such as keyboards, screens and printers, lets several programs run at once and provides the user interface, whether text commands or windows, icons and touch. Early computers had no operating system; operators loaded programs by hand. Batch systems appeared in the 1950s, and Unix, created at Bell Labs in 1969 by Ken Thompson and Dennis Ritchie, introduced influential ideas still used today. Microsoft’s MS-DOS (1981) and Windows (1985) came to dominate personal computers, while Apple’s Macintosh (1984) popularized the graphical interface. Linus Torvalds released the free, open-source Linux kernel in 1991; Linux now runs most web servers, supercomputers and, through Android, most smartphones. Today, Windows runs most desktop and laptop computers, followed by Apple’s macOS, while Google’s Android and Apple’s iOS run almost all smartphones. Operating systems are frequent targets of hackers, so updates are important.",
s:[["What an OS does","The kernel, the heart of the OS, decides which programs use the processor and memory, communicates with hardware through drivers and enforces security so programs cannot interfere with each other. The file system organizes data into files and folders."],["Unix and its descendants","Unix was written mostly in the C programming language, making it easy to move to new computers. Its descendants and look-alikes include Linux, Android, macOS and iOS, which is why many share commands and ideas."],["Linux and open source","Linux is open-source software that anyone can study, modify and share. It runs all of the world’s 500 fastest supercomputers, most web servers and many embedded devices such as routers and smart TVs."],["Updates and security","Operating system makers regularly release updates to fix security holes. In 2017, the WannaCry ransomware attack spread through computers running outdated versions of Windows, disrupting hospitals in Britain and businesses worldwide."]],
k:[["Main desktop systems","Windows, macOS, Linux"],["Main phone systems","Android, iOS"],["Unix created","1969, Bell Labs"],["Linux released","1991, Linus Torvalds"],["Core part","Kernel"]],
d:[["1969","Unix created at Bell Labs"],["1981","MS-DOS released"],["1984","Apple Macintosh with a graphical interface"],["1985","Windows 1.0 released"],["1991","Linux kernel released"],["2007","iPhone OS (iOS) introduced"],["2008","First Android phone released"]],
s2:[["The penguin","Linux’s mascot is a penguin named Tux, chosen by Linus Torvalds, who said he had once been bitten by a penguin at a zoo in Australia."],["The ‘blue screen of death’","When Windows encounters a serious error, it may display a blue error screen nicknamed the ‘blue screen of death’. In July 2024, a faulty security update from CrowdStrike caused about 8.5 million Windows computers to crash, disrupting airlines, banks and hospitals."]],
sp:["Grace Hopper","Computer pioneer Grace Hopper developed early compilers, programs that translate human-readable code into machine code, in the 1950s, laying foundations for programming languages and operating systems."],
g:[["Kernel","The core of an operating system"],["Driver","Software that lets the OS control a device"],["Open source","Software whose code anyone can use and change"],["Graphical interface","A screen with windows, icons and pointers"]],
f:["Linux runs all of the top 500 supercomputers.","Android is based on Linux.","Unix was created in 1969."],
fun:"Linus Torvalds announced Linux in 1991 as ‘just a hobby, won’t be big and professional’."});

ADDART({id:"officesoftware",t:"Spreadsheets and Word Processors",c:"Technology",i:"📊",w:"Spreadsheet",r:["comp","typewriter","operatingsystems","cloud","percent"],
p:[["Office tools","Word processors write documents and spreadsheets calculate with tables."],["Killer apps","VisiCalc, the first spreadsheet, made people buy personal computers."],["Used everywhere","Billions of people use office software for work and school."]],
l:"Word processors and spreadsheets are among the most widely used computer programs. A word processor lets users write, edit, format and print documents, replacing the typewriter; a spreadsheet arranges data in rows and columns of cells, with formulas that automatically recalculate results when numbers change. Early word processing machines appeared in the 1960s and 1970s, and programs such as WordStar (1978) and WordPerfect became popular on personal computers before Microsoft Word, first released in 1983, came to dominate. The first electronic spreadsheet, VisiCalc, created by Dan Bricklin and Bob Frankston for the Apple II in 1979, was a ‘killer app’ that convinced businesses to buy personal computers. It was followed by Lotus 1-2-3 and Microsoft Excel (1985). Today, Microsoft Office, Google Docs and Sheets, and Apple’s Pages and Numbers are used by billions of people, increasingly online, allowing several people to edit the same file at once. Spreadsheets are used for budgets, schedules, science and business, but errors in them have caused costly mistakes.",
s:[["How spreadsheets work","Each cell can contain text, a number or a formula, such as =SUM(A1:A10). When a value changes, all dependent formulas update instantly, letting users explore ‘what if’ questions. Charts turn data into graphs."],["VisiCalc","Dan Bricklin had the idea while a student at Harvard Business School, imagining a calculator that worked like a blackboard where changing one number updated all the others. VisiCalc sold hundreds of thousands of copies and made the Apple II a business tool."],["Word processors change writing","Word processing made editing easy, ended retyping whole pages and brought spelling and grammar checkers, fonts and templates to everyone. Some authors still write drafts by hand or on typewriters, but most writing now begins on screen."],["Spreadsheet errors","A 2013 analysis found a spreadsheet error in an influential economics paper that had been used to argue for government austerity. In 2020, England lost track of nearly 16,000 COVID-19 cases because an old Excel file format ran out of rows."]],
k:[["First spreadsheet","VisiCalc, 1979"],["Spreadsheet creators","Dan Bricklin and Bob Frankston"],["Microsoft Word","1983"],["Microsoft Excel","1985"],["Online editing","Google Docs and Sheets"]],
d:[["1978","WordStar word processor released"],["1979","VisiCalc released for the Apple II"],["1983","Lotus 1-2-3 and Microsoft Word released"],["1985","Microsoft Excel released"],["2006","Google Docs and Spreadsheets launched"]],
s2:[["Excel competitions","Since 2021, the Microsoft Excel World Championship has been held as an e-sport, with competitors racing to solve spreadsheet puzzles, broadcast online with commentators."],["Genes renamed","In 2020, scientists renamed 27 human genes, such as SEPT1 and MARCH1, because Excel automatically converted their names into dates, corrupting research data."]],
sp:["Not patented","VisiCalc was never patented, because software patents were rare at the time. Bricklin has said that if it had been, the spreadsheet industry might have developed very differently."],
g:[["Spreadsheet","A program for tables of data and calculations"],["Cell","One box in a spreadsheet grid"],["Formula","An instruction to calculate a value"],["Killer app","A program so useful it drives people to buy hardware"]],
f:["VisiCalc was the first spreadsheet program.","Scientists renamed genes because of Excel.","Microsoft Word was first released in 1983."],
fun:"Excel spreadsheets have been used to create art: Japanese retiree Tatsuo Horiuchi became famous for painting detailed landscapes using Excel’s drawing tools."});

ADDART({id:"nanotech",t:"Nanotechnology",c:"Technology",i:"🔬",w:"Nanotechnology",r:["atom","chip","mrna","microscopes","superconduct"],
p:[["The science of the very small","Nanotechnology works with materials at the scale of atoms and molecules."],["A nanometer is tiny","One nanometer is a billionth of a meter."],["Already everywhere","Nanotech is in computer chips, sunscreens, medicines and materials."]],
l:"Nanotechnology is the science and engineering of materials and devices at the nanoscale, roughly 1 to 100 nanometers; a nanometer is one billionth of a meter, about 100,000 times thinner than a human hair. At this scale, materials can behave very differently: gold particles can look red or purple, and carbon can form super-strong tubes and sheets. Physicist Richard Feynman inspired the field with a 1959 talk, ‘There’s Plenty of Room at the Bottom’, imagining manipulating individual atoms. The scanning tunneling microscope, invented in 1981, let scientists see and move single atoms; in 1989, IBM researchers spelled out ‘IBM’ with 35 xenon atoms. Discoveries such as buckyballs (1985), carbon nanotubes (1991) and graphene (2004) followed. Today, nanotechnology is used in computer chips with features only a few nanometers wide, sunscreens with zinc oxide nanoparticles, stain-resistant fabrics, scratch-resistant coatings, quantum dot TV screens and medicines, including the lipid nanoparticles that deliver mRNA COVID-19 vaccines. Scientists also study possible health and environmental risks of nanoparticles.",
s:[["Graphene","Graphene is a single layer of carbon atoms arranged in a honeycomb. Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov first isolated it in 2004 using sticky tape to peel layers from graphite, winning the 2010 Nobel Prize in Physics. It is extremely strong, flexible and conducts electricity and heat very well."],["Nanomedicine","Nanoparticles can deliver drugs directly to tumors, reducing side effects. The mRNA COVID-19 vaccines used lipid nanoparticles to protect the fragile mRNA and help it enter cells. Researchers are developing nanosensors to detect diseases early."],["Ancient nanotech","Medieval stained-glass makers unknowingly used gold and silver nanoparticles to produce vivid colors. The Roman Lycurgus Cup, from the 4th century, appears green in reflected light and red when lit from inside because of metal nanoparticles."],["Risks","Because nanoparticles are so small, they may enter the body through the lungs or skin and behave differently from larger particles. Regulators study their safety, and some products must label nanomaterials."]],
k:[["Scale","About 1–100 nanometers"],["A nanometer","A billionth of a meter"],["Inspired by","Richard Feynman’s 1959 talk"],["Key tool","Scanning tunneling microscope (1981)"],["Wonder material","Graphene (2004)"]],
d:[["1959","Feynman’s ‘Plenty of Room at the Bottom’ talk"],["1981","Scanning tunneling microscope invented"],["1985","Buckyballs discovered"],["1989","IBM spells its name with 35 atoms"],["1991","Carbon nanotubes described"],["2004","Graphene isolated"],["2010","Nobel Prize for graphene"]],
s2:[["Gray goo","In 1986, engineer K. Eric Drexler described tiny self-replicating machines and warned of a hypothetical ‘gray goo’ disaster in which they consumed everything. Most scientists consider such machines far beyond current or foreseeable technology."],["A Boy and His Atom","In 2013, IBM made the world’s smallest stop-motion film, ‘A Boy and His Atom’, by moving individual carbon monoxide molecules on a copper surface with a scanning tunneling microscope."]],
sp:["Nobel for molecular machines","The 2016 Nobel Prize in Chemistry was awarded to Jean-Pierre Sauvage, Fraser Stoddart and Ben Feringa for designing molecular machines, including a ‘nanocar’ with four molecular wheels."],
g:[["Nanometer","One billionth of a meter"],["Nanoparticle","A particle between 1 and 100 nanometers"],["Graphene","A one-atom-thick sheet of carbon"],["Scanning tunneling microscope","A microscope that images single atoms"]],
f:["A nanometer is a billionth of a meter.","Graphene was first isolated using sticky tape.","mRNA vaccines use lipid nanoparticles."],
fun:"IBM researchers once wrote their company name using just 35 individual xenon atoms."});

ADDART({id:"airships",t:"Airships",c:"Technology",i:"🎈",w:"Airship",r:["flight","drones","hydrogen","wwi","fluids"],
p:[["Lighter than air","Airships float using gases lighter than air, such as helium."],["The age of Zeppelins","Giant German Zeppelins carried passengers across oceans in the 1930s."],["The Hindenburg disaster","The 1937 Hindenburg fire ended the airship era."]],
l:"An airship, or dirigible, is a powered, steerable aircraft that floats because it is filled with a gas lighter than air, such as hydrogen or helium. Unlike balloons, airships have engines, propellers and rudders. French engineer Henri Giffard flew the first powered airship in 1852. German Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin built rigid airships with metal frames covered in fabric, first flown in 1900, and Zeppelins carried passengers before World War I and bombed Britain during the war. In the 1920s and 1930s, giant airships crossed oceans in luxury: the Graf Zeppelin flew around the world in 1929 and made regular flights to South America. On 6 May 1937, the hydrogen-filled Hindenburg caught fire while landing at Lakehurst, New Jersey, killing 36 people; filmed and broadcast, the disaster ended passenger airship travel. Today, small helium-filled blimps are used for advertising, camera views of sports events and surveillance. Companies are developing new airships for cargo and tourism because they can carry heavy loads with low fuel use.",
s:[["Types","Rigid airships have an internal metal frame containing gas cells. Non-rigid airships, called blimps, keep their shape only by gas pressure. Semi-rigid airships have a partial frame, like the modern Zeppelin NT."],["The Graf Zeppelin","The LZ 127 Graf Zeppelin flew about 1.7 million kilometers in 590 flights without injuring a passenger, including a round-the-world flight in August 1929 that took 21 days, with stops in Tokyo, Los Angeles and New York."],["Why the Hindenburg burned","The Hindenburg was designed for non-flammable helium, but the United States, which controlled most of the world’s helium, refused to export it, so it used hydrogen. Investigators believe a static electric spark ignited leaking hydrogen. Remarkably, 62 of the 97 people on board survived."],["Modern airships","In 2023, Pathfinder 1, a large rigid airship built by LTA Research and funded by Google co-founder Sergey Brin, began test flights. Other companies plan airships to carry cargo to remote areas without roads, though large airships remain sensitive to wind and weather."]],
k:[["Lift gas","Hydrogen or helium"],["First powered airship","Henri Giffard, 1852"],["Rigid airship pioneer","Ferdinand von Zeppelin"],["Hindenburg disaster","6 May 1937"],["Non-rigid type","Blimp"]],
d:[["1852","Giffard flies the first powered airship"],["1900","First Zeppelin flight"],["1915","Zeppelins bomb London"],["1929","Graf Zeppelin flies around the world"],["1937","Hindenburg disaster"],["2023","Pathfinder 1 begins test flights"]],
s2:[["‘Oh, the humanity!’","Radio reporter Herbert Morrison’s emotional description of the Hindenburg fire, including the words ‘Oh, the humanity!’, became one of the most famous broadcasts in history, later synchronized with newsreel film."],["The Empire State Building’s mooring mast","The spire of the Empire State Building, completed in 1931, was designed as a mooring mast for airships, but strong winds made docking impractical, and it was never used for passengers."]],
sp:["The R101","Britain’s airship R101 crashed in France on its first overseas voyage in October 1930, killing 48 people, ending Britain’s airship program."],
g:[["Dirigible","A steerable airship"],["Blimp","An airship without a rigid frame"],["Helium","A light, non-flammable gas"],["Mooring mast","A tower to which airships are tied"]],
f:["The Hindenburg disaster ended passenger airship travel.","The Graf Zeppelin flew around the world in 1929.","Blimps keep their shape by gas pressure."],
fun:"The Hindenburg had a smoking room on board, sealed and pressurized to keep out hydrogen, despite being filled with explosive gas."});

ADDART({id:"hovercraft",t:"Hovercraft",c:"Technology",i:"🛥️",w:"Hovercraft",r:["ships","flight","tunnels","ww2","vacuumcleaner"],
p:[["Riding on air","Hovercraft travel on a cushion of air over land, water, mud and ice."],["A British invention","Christopher Cockerell patented the hovercraft in 1955."],["Across the Channel","Large hovercraft carried cars across the English Channel for decades."]],
l:"A hovercraft, or air-cushion vehicle, is a craft that travels over land, water, mud, ice and other surfaces on a cushion of air created by powerful fans blowing air underneath it, contained by a flexible rubber skirt. Because it floats just above the surface, it meets little friction and can cross terrain where boats and wheeled vehicles cannot, such as swamps, sandbanks and ice. British engineer Christopher Cockerell developed the modern hovercraft, testing the idea with a vacuum cleaner and two cans, and patented it in 1955. The first full-size hovercraft, the SR.N1, crossed the English Channel on 25 July 1959, the 50th anniversary of Louis Blériot’s flight. From 1968 to 2000, giant SR.N4 hovercraft carried passengers and cars across the Channel in about 30 minutes, until the Channel Tunnel and faster ferries replaced them. Hovercraft are used today by rescue services, militaries for landing troops and vehicles on beaches, and on some passenger routes, such as between Southsea and the Isle of Wight. Hobbyists race small hovercraft.",
s:[["How it works","Fans push air beneath the craft, raising it slightly; the skirt traps the air, creating a cushion. Separate propellers or fans, often at the back, drive it forward, and rudders steer. Hovercraft can be hard to control in strong winds because they slide easily."],["Cockerell’s experiment","Cockerell put a smaller can inside a larger one, blew air from a vacuum cleaner between them and measured how much force the air cushion produced on a kitchen scale. He found that the ‘momentum curtain’ produced much more lift than air blown directly."],["The Channel hovercraft","The SR.N4, the largest civil hovercraft, carried up to about 418 passengers and 60 cars at speeds of up to about 80 kilometers per hour. Crossings took about 30 minutes, but the ride could be bumpy and noisy, and services stopped in 2000."],["Military and rescue","The US Navy’s Landing Craft Air Cushion carries tanks and troops from ships onto beaches. Rescue services use small hovercraft on mudflats, frozen lakes and floods where boats cannot reach."]],
k:[["Travels on","A cushion of air"],["Inventor","Christopher Cockerell"],["Patent","1955"],["First Channel crossing","SR.N1, 25 July 1959"],["Cross-Channel service","1968–2000"]],
d:[["1955","Cockerell patents the hovercraft"],["1959","SR.N1 crosses the English Channel"],["1965","Hovercraft passenger service to the Isle of Wight begins"],["1968","SR.N4 cross-Channel service begins"],["2000","Cross-Channel hovercraft service ends"]],
s2:[["‘My hovercraft is full of eels’","A famous Monty Python sketch features a tourist with a badly translated phrasebook who says, ‘My hovercraft is full of eels’. The phrase became a popular example in language-learning circles."],["Hovering on ice","Hovercraft are used in Canada to break ice on rivers to prevent flooding: the air cushion creates waves under the ice that make it crack."]],
sp:["World Hovercraft Championships","Hovercraft racing is a sport with national and world championships. Racers drive small hovercraft over grass, water and obstacle courses at speeds above 100 kilometers per hour."],
g:[["Air cushion","A layer of pressurized air underneath a craft"],["Skirt","The flexible barrier holding the air cushion"],["Friction","Resistance between surfaces"],["Amphibious","Able to operate on land and water"]],
f:["Hovercraft can cross land, water and ice.","Cockerell tested the idea with a vacuum cleaner and cans.","Hovercraft crossed the English Channel from 1968 to 2000."],
fun:"Christopher Cockerell first tested his hovercraft idea using a vacuum cleaner, a cat food tin, a coffee tin and kitchen scales."});

ADDART({id:"farmmachinery",t:"Tractors and Farm Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🚜",w:"Agricultural machinery",r:["agri","urbanfarm","indrev","drones","robot"],
p:[["Machines in the fields","Tractors and harvesters do the work once done by people and animals."],["A food revolution","Farm machines let fewer farmers feed many more people."],["Smart farming","Modern machines use GPS, sensors and autonomy."]],
l:"Farm machinery includes the tractors, plows, seed drills, harvesters and other machines used to grow crops and raise livestock. For thousands of years, farming relied on human and animal muscle. Inventions of the Agricultural and Industrial Revolutions began to change this: Jethro Tull’s seed drill (about 1701), Andrew Meikle’s threshing machine (1786) and Cyrus McCormick’s mechanical reaper (patented 1834). Steam traction engines were used from the mid-19th century, and gasoline tractors appeared in the 1890s; the mass-produced Fordson tractor of 1917 made tractors affordable, and Harry Ferguson’s three-point hitch of the 1920s and 1930s made them far more useful. The combine harvester, which reaps, threshes and cleans grain in one operation, became widespread in the 20th century. Mechanization allowed far fewer people to produce far more food: about 40 percent of Americans worked on farms in 1900, compared with about 1 percent today. Modern machines use GPS guidance, sensors and computers to plant and spray precisely, and self-driving tractors and robots are emerging. In many poorer countries, much farm work is still done by hand.",
s:[["The tractor","Tractors pull and power other machines through a power take-off shaft. Today’s largest tractors have more than 600 horsepower, rubber tracks and air-conditioned cabs with computer screens. Small tractors and two-wheeled walking tractors are common in Asia."],["The combine harvester","A combine cuts grain stalks, separates the grain from the husks and straw, and stores it in a tank, combining jobs that once needed many workers. Modern combines can harvest dozens of hectares a day and map yields across a field."],["Precision agriculture","GPS steering keeps tractors on precise paths, avoiding overlaps. Sensors and drones measure soil and crop health so that fertilizers, water and pesticides can be applied only where needed, saving money and reducing pollution."],["Right to repair","Modern tractors contain software that restricts repairs to authorized dealers. Farmers in the United States campaigned for the ‘right to repair’ their own machines, and in 2023 the manufacturer John Deere agreed to give more access to repair tools."]],
k:[["Seed drill","Jethro Tull, about 1701"],["Mechanical reaper","Cyrus McCormick, 1834"],["Mass-produced tractor","Fordson, 1917"],["Combine harvester","Reaps, threshes and cleans grain"],["US farm workers","About 40% in 1900, about 1% today"]],
d:[["c. 1701","Jethro Tull’s seed drill"],["1834","McCormick patents his reaper"],["1892","John Froelich builds a gasoline tractor"],["1917","Fordson tractor mass-produced"],["1926","Ferguson’s three-point hitch patented"],["2022","Self-driving tractor unveiled for sale"]],
s2:[["Horses and tractors","In 1920, the United States had about 25 million horses and mules, many working on farms. As tractors spread, their numbers fell dramatically, freeing land once used to grow animal feed for crops for people."],["Lamborghini","Ferruccio Lamborghini made his fortune building tractors in Italy after World War II before founding his famous sports car company in 1963; the company still makes tractors under a separate brand."]],
sp:["Walking tractors","Two-wheeled ‘walking tractors’, or power tillers, steered by a person walking behind, are widely used on small rice farms in Asia, including in Thailand, Vietnam and Bangladesh."],
g:[["Mechanization","Replacing human and animal labor with machines"],["Combine harvester","A machine that harvests and threshes grain"],["Power take-off","A shaft that transfers power to other machines"],["Precision agriculture","Farming using data to target inputs"]],
f:["About 1% of Americans now work on farms.","The combine harvester does several jobs at once.","Modern tractors use GPS to steer."],
fun:"Lamborghini started as a tractor company before making supercars."});

ADDART({id:"constructionmachines",t:"Cranes and Construction Machines",c:"Technology",i:"🏗️",w:"Heavy equipment",r:["skyscrapers","bridges","tunnels","concrete","machines"],
p:[["Building big","Cranes, excavators and bulldozers do heavy lifting and digging."],["Ancient roots","The Romans used human-powered cranes called treadwheels."],["Giant machines","The largest cranes can lift thousands of metric tons."]],
l:"Construction machines, or heavy equipment, are powerful vehicles and devices used to build roads, buildings, bridges, tunnels and dams. They include cranes for lifting heavy loads, excavators and backhoes for digging, bulldozers for pushing earth, loaders, dump trucks, concrete mixers, pavers and tunnel boring machines. Lifting machines date back to ancient times: the ancient Greeks invented cranes around 500 BC, and the Romans used huge treadwheel cranes powered by people walking inside giant wheels. Steam power brought steam shovels in the 19th century, which dug much of the Panama Canal. The internal combustion engine, caterpillar tracks and hydraulics transformed construction in the 20th century: Benjamin Holt’s crawler tractor of 1904 led to bulldozers, and hydraulic excavators became common from the 1950s and 1960s. Today, tower cranes are a familiar sight on city skylines, and the largest land-based cranes can lift several thousand metric tons. Construction machines are increasingly electric, remote-controlled or automated, reducing emissions and keeping workers safer.",
s:[["Cranes","Tower cranes, anchored to concrete bases, grow taller as a building rises by adding sections, a process called climbing. Mobile cranes on trucks or tracks move between sites. Floating cranes on ships lift bridge sections and offshore structures; some can lift more than 10,000 metric tons."],["Excavators and bulldozers","Hydraulic excavators use pressurized oil in cylinders to move a boom, arm and bucket with great force and precision. Bulldozers push soil with a large blade. Caterpillar tracks spread a machine’s weight, letting it work on soft ground."],["Tunnel boring machines","Tunnel boring machines are giant rotating cutting heads up to about 17 meters wide that dig tunnels while installing concrete lining segments behind them. They dug the Channel Tunnel, the Gotthard Base Tunnel and many subway lines."],["Safety and automation","Construction is one of the most dangerous industries. Sensors, cameras and remote controls help prevent accidents. In Japan, where workers are scarce, companies use self-driving excavators and dump trucks on some sites."]],
k:[["Main machines","Cranes, excavators, bulldozers, loaders"],["Ancient crane","Roman treadwheel crane"],["Steam shovel use","Panama Canal"],["Key technology","Hydraulics"],["Largest cranes","Lift thousands of metric tons"]],
d:[["c. 500 BC","Cranes invented in ancient Greece"],["1839","William Otis patents the steam shovel"],["1904","Benjamin Holt tests a crawler tractor"],["1920s","Bulldozer blades fitted to tractors"],["1950s","Hydraulic excavators spread"],["1988","Tunnel boring begins on the Channel Tunnel"]],
s2:[["The steam shovel and the Panama Canal","About 100 steam shovels dug out the Panama Canal between 1904 and 1914, removing millions of cubic meters of earth and rock each year."],["Construction toys","Toy cranes, diggers and dump trucks are among the most popular children’s toys, and in some places adults can pay to operate real excavators at ‘dig parks’."]],
sp:["Big Carl","Big Carl, a ring crane used to build the Hinkley Point C nuclear power station in England, is one of the world’s largest land-based cranes, able to lift about 5,000 metric tons and reaching 250 meters high."],
g:[["Hydraulics","Using pressurized liquid to transmit force"],["Excavator","A digging machine with a boom and bucket"],["Bulldozer","A tracked tractor with a pushing blade"],["Tower crane","A tall crane fixed to the ground"]],
f:["The Romans used human-powered treadwheel cranes.","Steam shovels dug the Panama Canal.","Some floating cranes lift over 10,000 metric tons."],
fun:"Tower cranes ‘climb’ by lifting new sections of their own tower into place, growing taller as a skyscraper rises."});

ADDART({id:"wristwatches",t:"Wristwatches",c:"Technology",i:"⌚",w:"Watch",r:["clocks","timephys","wearables","switzerlandcountry","wwi"],
p:[["Time on your wrist","Wristwatches let people carry the time with them."],["Popular after World War I","Soldiers found wristwatches more practical than pocket watches."],["From gears to quartz","Quartz and digital watches transformed the industry in the 1970s."]],
l:"A wristwatch is a small timepiece worn on a strap around the wrist. Portable watches developed from spring-driven clocks in 16th-century Europe and were first worn as pendants or carried in pockets. Wristwatches, or wristlets, were at first considered jewelry for women; one of the earliest known was made by Breguet for the Queen of Naples in 1810. During the Boer War and especially World War I, soldiers found it impractical to pull out pocket watches during battle and strapped watches to their wrists, and after the war wristwatches became popular with men. Mechanical watches keep time using a mainspring, gears and a balance wheel; automatic watches wind themselves from the wearer’s movement. In 1969, the Japanese company Seiko launched the Astron, the first quartz wristwatch, which used a vibrating quartz crystal to keep far more accurate time. Cheap quartz and digital watches caused the ‘quartz crisis’ that devastated the Swiss watch industry in the 1970s and 1980s, which recovered with brands like Swatch and luxury mechanical watches. Smartwatches now combine timekeeping with fitness tracking and messaging.",
s:[["Mechanical watches","In a mechanical watch, a wound mainspring releases energy through gears, controlled by an escapement and balance wheel that swings back and forth several times a second, making the ticking sound. Jewels, usually synthetic rubies, reduce friction at key points."],["Quartz","A quartz crystal vibrates exactly 32,768 times per second when electricity is applied. An electronic circuit counts the vibrations to measure seconds. Quartz watches are accurate to within a few seconds a month, far better than most mechanical watches."],["The quartz crisis","Between 1970 and 1983, the number of people employed in Swiss watchmaking fell from about 90,000 to about 30,000 as cheap quartz watches from Japan and elsewhere flooded the market. The colorful, affordable Swatch, launched in 1983, helped rescue the industry."],["Watches in space","The Omega Speedmaster was qualified by NASA in 1965 and worn on the Moon during Apollo 11, earning the nickname ‘Moonwatch’. On Apollo 13, astronaut Jack Swigert used one to time a crucial engine burn."]],
k:[["Early wristwatch","Breguet, 1810"],["Popular with men","After World War I"],["First quartz watch","Seiko Astron, 1969"],["Quartz frequency","32,768 vibrations per second"],["Industry shock","The quartz crisis (1970s–1980s)"]],
d:[["1810","Breguet makes a wristwatch for the Queen of Naples"],["1914–1918","Soldiers adopt wristwatches in World War I"],["1923","First self-winding wristwatch patented"],["1969","Seiko Astron, the first quartz wristwatch"],["1972","First digital LED watch, the Pulsar"],["1983","Swatch launched"],["2015","Apple Watch released"]],
s2:[["The ‘Moonwatch’","Omega watches were tested by NASA in 1964–65 with extreme heat, cold, shock and vacuum. Several brands failed; the Speedmaster passed and became standard issue for astronauts."],["Smartwatches","Smartwatches such as the Apple Watch, released in 2015, track heart rate, steps and sleep, show messages and can detect falls or irregular heart rhythms. By the 2020s, more smartwatches than Swiss watches were being sold each year."]],
sp:["10:10","Watches in advertisements are almost always set to about 10:10, because the hands frame the brand logo and form a shape resembling a smile."],
g:[["Mainspring","A coiled spring that powers a mechanical watch"],["Balance wheel","A wheel that swings to regulate time"],["Quartz","A crystal that vibrates at a precise frequency"],["Escapement","The mechanism that releases energy in steps"]],
f:["Wristwatches became popular with men after World War I.","Quartz crystals vibrate 32,768 times a second.","An Omega watch went to the Moon."],
fun:"Watches in advertisements are usually set to 10:10 because the hands look like a smile and frame the logo."});
